          %h2#idea Idea
          %p
            In
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/logic"} logic
            %strong excluded middle
            states that every
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/truth+value"} truth value
            is either
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/true"} true
            or
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/false"} false
            (
            %a{:href => "#AristotleMetaphysics"}> Aristotle, MP1011b24
            ). (This is sometimes called the ‘
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/axiom"}> axiom
            ’ or ‘law’ of excluded middle, either to emphasise that it is or is not optional; ‘principle’ is a relatively neutral term.) One of the many meanings of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/classical+logic"} classical logic
            is to emphasise that this principle holds in the logic; in contrast, it fails in
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/intuitionistic+logic"} intuitionistic logic
          %p
            The principle of excluded middle (hereafter, PEM), as a statement about truth values themselves, is accepted by nearly all mathematicians (
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/classical+mathematics"}> classical mathematics
            ); those who doubt or deny it are a distinct minority, the
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/constructive+mathematics"} constructivists
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/internalization"} internalises
            mathematics in
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/categories"} categories
            other than
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Set"} the category of sets
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/internal+logic"} internally
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/internal+logic"} internal logic
            = succeed ";" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Boolean+category"} Boolean
          %p
            Although the term ‘excluded middle’ (sometimes even
            = succeed ")" do
              %em excluded third
            %em externally
            but may still be two-valued
            = succeed "." do
              %em internally
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/categorical+logic"} categorical logic
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/subterminal+objects"} subterminal objects
            is in general independent of whether it is Boolean; instead, the category is Boolean iff the statement that it has exactly two subterminal objects holds in its
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/internal+logic"} internal logic
            (which is in general independent of whether that statement is true).
          %p
            In fact, intuitionistic logic proves that there is no truth value that is neither true nor false; in this sense, the possibility of a ‘middle’ or ‘third’ truth value is still ‘excluded’. But since the relevant
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/de+Morgan+law"} de Morgan law
            fails in intuitionistic logic, we may not conclude that every truth value is either true or false, which is the actual PEM.
          %h2#in_set_theory In set theory
          %p
            In
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/material+set+theory"} material set theory
            %a{:href => "#Shulman18"} Shulman 18
            makes the distinction between
            = succeed "," do
              %strong full classical logic
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/material+set+theory"} material set theory
            = succeed "," do
              %strong
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi Δ
                        %mn 0
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Delta_0
                \-classical logic
            = succeed "-formulae." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi Δ
                      %mn 0
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Delta_0
            = succeed "-formulae" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi Δ
                      %mn 0
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Delta_0
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantifier"} quantifier
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Q
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Q
            has to be bounded by the membership relation, i.e.
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo ∃
                    %mi x
                    %mo ∈
                    %mi y
                    %mo .
                    %mi P
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi x
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \exists x \in y.P(x)
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo ∃
                    %mi x
                    %mo .
                    %mi x
                    %mo ∈
                    %mi y
                    %mo ∧
                    %mi P
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi x
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \exists x.x \in y \wedge P(x)
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo ∀
                    %mi x
                    %mo ∈
                    %mi y
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \forall x \in y
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo ∀
                    %mi x
                    %mo .
                    %mi x
                    %mo ∈
                    %mi y
                    %mo ⇒
                    %mi P
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi x
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \forall x. x \in y \implies P(x)
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/structural+set+theory"} structural set theory
            = succeed "-classical" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi Δ
                      %mn 0
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Delta_0
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/material+set+theory"} material set theory
            is the difference between defining
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/structural+set+theory"} structural set theory
            as a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/well-pointed+topos"} well-pointed
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Boolean+topos"} Boolean topos
            in
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/classical+logic"} classical logic
            and in
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/intuitionistic+logic"} intuitionistic logic
            respectively.
          %h2#in_type_theory In type theory
          %p
            In
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/dependent+type+theory"} dependent type theory
          %h3#truncated_excluded_middle Truncated excluded middle
          %p
            The statement which directly corresponds to excluded middle in logic states that given a type
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi P
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} P
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi P
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} P
            is a
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/mere+proposition"} mere proposition
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi P
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} P
            or the negation of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi P
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} P
            holds
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mfrac
                    %mrow
                      %mi Γ
                      %mo ⊢
                      %mi P
                      %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                      %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} type
                    %mrow
                      %mi Γ
                      %mo ⊢
                      %msub
                        %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} lem
                        %mi P
                      %mo :
                      %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} isProp
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi P
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo →
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi P
                      %mo ∨
                      %mo ¬
                      %mi P
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \frac{\Gamma \vdash P \; \mathrm{type}}{\Gamma \vdash \mathrm{lem}_P:\mathrm{isProp}(P) \to (P \vee \neg P)}
          %p
            where
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} isProp
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathrm{isProp}
            is the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/isProp"} isProp
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/modality"} modality
            commonly defined as
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ∏
                      %mrow
                        %mi x
                        %mo :
                        %mi P
                    %msub
                      %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ∏
                      %mrow
                        %mi y
                        %mo :
                        %mi P
                    %msub
                      %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} Id
                      %mi P
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi x
                    %mo ,
                    %mi y
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \prod_{x:P} \prod_{y:P} \mathrm{Id}_P(x, y)
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo ¬
                  %mi P
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \neg P
            is simply the the function type
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi P
                  %mo →
                  %mi 𝟘
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} P \to \mathbb{0}
            into the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/empty+type"} empty type
            and
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/disjunction"} disjunction
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi A
                  %mo ∨
                  %mi B
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A \vee B
            is the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/bracket+type"} bracket type
            of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/sum+type"} sum type
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                    %mi A
                    %mo +
                    %mi B
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} [A + B]
          %p
            If the dependent type theory has
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/type+variables"} type variables
            and
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/impredicative+polymorphism"} impredicative polymorphism
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mfrac
                    %mrow
                      %mi Γ
                      %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                      %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} ctx
                    %mrow
                      %mi Γ
                      %mo ⊢
                      %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} lem
                      %mo :
                      %mi Π
                      %mi P
                      %mo .
                      %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} isProp
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi P
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo →
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi P
                      %mo ∨
                      %mo ¬
                      %mi P
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \frac{\Gamma \; \mathrm{ctx}}{\Gamma \vdash \mathrm{lem}:\Pi P.\mathrm{isProp}(P) \to (P \vee \neg P)}
          %p
            Similarly, if the dependent type theory additionally has a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Russell+type+of+all+propositions"} Russell type of all propositions
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Ω
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Omega
            or a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Tarski+type+of+all+propositions"} Tarski type of all propositions
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi Ω
                    %mo ,
                    %msub
                      %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} El
                      %mi Ω
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (\Omega, \mathrm{El}_\Omega)
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mfrac
                    %mrow
                      %mi Γ
                      %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                      %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} ctx
                    %mrow
                      %mi Γ
                      %mo ⊢
                      %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} doubleneg
                      %mo :
                      %munder
                        %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ∏
                        %mrow
                          %mi P
                          %mo :
                          %mi Ω
                      %mi P
                      %mo ∨
                      %mo ¬
                      %mi P
                  %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} Russell
                  %mspace{:width => "2em"}
                  %mfrac
                    %mrow
                      %mi Γ
                      %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                      %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} ctx
                    %mrow
                      %mi Γ
                      %mo ⊢
                      %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} doubleneg
                      %mo :
                      %munder
                        %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ∏
                        %mrow
                          %mi P
                          %mo :
                          %mi Ω
                      %msub
                        %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} El
                        %mi Ω
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi P
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo ∨
                      %mo ¬
                      %msub
                        %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} El
                        %mi Ω
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi P
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} Tarski
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \frac{\Gamma \; \mathrm{ctx}}{\Gamma \vdash \mathrm{doubleneg}:\prod_{P:\Omega} P \vee \neg P}\mathrm{Russell} \qquad \frac{\Gamma \; \mathrm{ctx}}{\Gamma \vdash \mathrm{doubleneg}:\prod_{P:\Omega} \mathrm{El}_\Omega(P) \vee \neg \mathrm{El}_\Omega(P)}\mathrm{Tarski}
          %p
